{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.one_measurement_near_optimum","record":{"id":"circuit.one_measurement_near_optimum","kind":"claim","label":"One measured sample approaches the SK optimum with high joint probability","status":"unverified","statement":"For every epsilon>0 there exist a finite depth p and fixed deterministic real angles, independent of n and the Gaussian disorder, such that Pr_{J,sigma}[H_n(sigma)/n >= P_star-epsilon] tends to 1 as n tends to infinity; sigma is one computational-basis measurement of the corresponding QAOA state.","assumption_ids":["assumptions.qaoa_model_and_limits"],"evidence_ids":["concentration_source_warrant_pending"],"citation_ids":["qaoa_main_theorem","fggz2022_fixed_depth"],"boundary":"Ordinary derived consequence of the cited thermodynamic-first QAOA theorem and the fixed-word moment theorem. No formalizes edge or kernel acceptance. This is a joint-disorder-and-measurement asymptotic statement, not a guarantee on every instance, a finite-size rate, a uniform p(n) claim, or an efficient angle-selection algorithm.","locator":"Derived in the long reasoning record, What is averaged, and a fixed-word probability consequence (#fixed-word-probability); premise locations are QAOA Theorem thm:main and FGGZ section 4 equations (13)–(14)."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.one_measurement_near_optimum","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/qaoa-quantifiers","anchor":"qaoa-quantifiers","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/qaoa-quantifiers","anchor":"qaoa-quantifiers","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n\n<a id=\"fixed-word-probability\"></a>\n\n### 11.2 What is averaged, and a fixed-word probability consequence\n\nThe probability conclusion here uses an additional fixed-word second-moment\ntheorem, beyond the expected-value construction of §§8.1–8.6.\nThe theorem in §8 is an existence statement about limiting expected\nenergy. There are two random choices behind that expectation. First the\ncouplings $J$ are sampled; then a computational-basis measurement of the\nstate produces a spin string $\\Sigma$. For the single word $W$ already\nchosen, put\n$$\nE_n(J,\\Sigma)=H_{n,J}(\\Sigma)/n,\\qquad\n\\overline E_n(J)=\\mathbb E[E_n(J,\\Sigma)\\mid J].\n$$\nThe quantity defining $v_W$ is $\\mathbb E_J\\overline E_n(J)$.\nIt is neither the energy of every measured string nor a worst-disorder\nguarantee. The temporary Gaussian labels used during synthesis are not\nan extra source of randomness in the final angle vectors.\n\nFarhi, Goldstone, Gutmann and Zhou supply that additional input. They prove, with\nthe same Hamiltonian and gate-sign convention, that for every fixed word\n$$\n\\mathbb E_{J,\\Sigma}E_n\\longrightarrow v_W,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{J,\\Sigma}E_n^2\\longrightarrow v_W^2.\n$$\nTheir second expression is the quantum expectation of $(C_{n,J}/n)^2$\naveraged over disorder, not merely the square of the conditional mean.\nConsequently\n$$\n\\mathbb E_{J,\\Sigma}(E_n-v_W)^2\\longrightarrow0,\n\\qquad\n\\Pr_{J,\\Sigma}(|E_n-v_W|>s)\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad(s>0).\n$$\nThe law of total variance separates what vanishes:\n$$\n\\mathbb E(E_n-v_W)^2=\n\\mathbb E_J\\operatorname{Var}(E_n\\mid J)\n +\\mathbb E_J(\\overline E_n(J)-v_W)^2.\n$$\nBoth terms are nonnegative and hence tend to zero. Concentration of the\nconditional mean alone would not control a measured string.\nThis is the elementary second-moment deduction from [Farhi et al.,\narXiv:1910.08187v4, §4, equations (13)–(22)][fggz].\n\nNow run the circuit construction with accuracy $\\varepsilon/2$ and\nfreeze its resulting word. Since $v_W\\ge P_*-\\varepsilon/2$,\n$$\n\\Pr_{J,\\Sigma}\\{E_n<P_*-\\varepsilon\\}\n\\le \\frac{4}{\\varepsilon^2}\n       \\mathbb E_{J,\\Sigma}(E_n-v_W)^2\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\nThis is a consequence of the expected-value construction **together\nwith** the external second-moment theorem. It is not a clause of either\nselected formal interface. If\n$b_n(J)=\\Pr(E_n<P_*-\\varepsilon\\mid J)$, then for every fixed\n$\\tau>0$,\n$$\n\\Pr_J\\{b_n(J)>\\tau\\}\n\\le\\mathbb E_J b_n(J)/\\tau\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\nThus the same word also has small conditional measurement-failure\nprobability for most disorder instances, in this precise sense. No\nclaim is made for every instance, for arbitrarily varying depths, or\nwith a quantitative size threshold.\n\nFor $K$ fresh state preparations at the same disorder instance, the\nconditional failures are independent, so their joint failure probability\nis $\\mathbb E_J b_n(J)^K$, not $(\\mathbb E_J b_n(J))^K$.\nA fraction $\\alpha$ of instances that always fail gives $\\alpha$ for\nthe former at every $K$. A bound $b_n(J)\\le\\tau$ on a particular\ninstance does give $\\tau^K$ there. Repeatedly measuring an already\ncollapsed state is a different experiment.\n\n","span":{"start_byte":221348,"end_byte":224449,"start_line":4784,"end_line":4861},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=qaoa-quantifiers","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=qaoa-quantifiers"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=circuit.one_measurement_near_optimum&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=circuit.one_measurement_near_optimum&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=circuit.one_measurement_near_optimum&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=circuit.one_measurement_near_optimum&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"field:fa2a27dafd4954120f3e5ca72d5022caaad16fc34615ea1710016d306d4680c5","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.one_measurement_near_optimum","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/assumptions.qaoa_model_and_limits","kind":"assumption_ids","semantic_class":"explicit_assumption_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.one_measurement_near_optimum","source_field":"assumption_ids"},{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc28